menu "Detector Wi-Fi capture"

    config FY_WIFI_COUNTRY
        string "Wi-Fi country code"
        default "01"
        help
            The regulatory domain, which sets the channels the radio may
            tune to and the Wi-Fi dashboard's transmit rules. "01" is
            ESP-IDF's world-safe default: channels 1-11, as in the US and
            Canada. Set your country (for example GB or DE) to also sweep
            channels 12 and 13 where they are allowed. Codes ESP-IDF
            accepts: AT AU BE BG BR CA CH CN CY CZ DE DK EE ES FI FR GB GR HK
            HR HU IE IN IS IT JP KR LI LT LU LV MT MX NL NO NZ PL PT RO SE SI
            SK TW US.

    config FY_WIFI_CHANNELS_UPSTREAM
        bool "Sweep only channels 11, 6, and 1 (upstream)"
        default n
        help
            Off: sweep every channel the country allows (1-11 by default,
            1-13 in most of the world), highest first. Flock cameras probe
            on every channel as they step through them, but their own
            traffic and SoftAP sit on whatever channel their network uses.
            On: upstream flock-you's three non-overlapping channels, which
            return to each channel sooner but never hear the others.

    config FY_WIFI_DWELL_MS
        int "Time on each Wi-Fi channel (ms)"
        default 250
        range 100 1000
        help
            250 ms is two of the cameras' observed 125 ms channel steps,
            as upstream uses. A full sweep of 11 channels takes 2.75 s.

endmenu

menu "Detector signatures"

    config FY_SIG_MOTOROLA
        bool "Match Motorola Solutions OUIs and BLE IDs"
        default y
        help
            Motorola Solutions' IEEE blocks and Bluetooth IDs cover its
            whole product line: two-way radios carried by retail, school,
            and venue staff as much as police. Matches are labeled
            possible. Turn off if they are noise where you use the device.

    config FY_SIG_SIERRA_WIRELESS
        bool "Match Sierra Wireless OUIs"
        default y
        help
            In-vehicle routers used by police fleets, and by many civilian
            fleets too. Matches are labeled possible.

    config FY_SIG_CRADLEPOINT
        bool "Match Cradlepoint OUIs"
        default n
        help
            Off by default, as in the parity reference: its field testing
            found no police vehicles and a steady stream of school buses
            and cellular traffic cameras.

    config FY_SIG_RADIO_OUIS_ANY_FRAME
        bool "Match Flock radio-module OUIs on any frame"
        default n
        help
            The community OUI list for Flock cameras is mostly Lite-On,
            Silicon Labs, Espressif, Samsung, and USI radio-module blocks,
            which also ship in laptops, phones, and IoT gear. Off: they are
            matched only in wildcard probe requests, where the IE
            fingerprint separates a camera from a laptop. On: upstream's
            behaviour, matching them as transmitter, receiver, or BSSID in
            any frame (labeled possible).

endmenu

menu "Detector output"

    config FY_SERIAL_JSON
        bool "Print upstream flock-you dashboard JSON for each reported match"
        default y
        help
            Each reported match is also printed on the console as one JSON
            line in the upstream flock-you format, which its Flask dashboard
            (api/flockyou.py) reads over USB serial. Lines carry no location;
            the dashboard adds the host's GPS. Log lines that are not JSON
            are ignored by the dashboard.

endmenu
